    "knowledge_point": "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity: Case With Alternating Signs",
    "problem": "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.99)^{7}.$ Give your answer to four decimal places.",
    "worked_solution": "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.01$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.99)^{7} & =(1+(-0.01))^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(-0.01)+21(-0.01)^{2} \\\\  & =1-7(0.01)+21(0.000\\,1) \\\\  & =1-0.07+0.002\\,1 \\\\  & =0.932\\,1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto four decimal places.",
